1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,834
Hi, I'm Ryan Rover.

2
00:00:02,014 --> 00:00:03,570
Here's something strange.

3
00:00:03,750 --> 00:00:06,465
Americans earn more today than almost any

4
00:00:06,465 --> 00:00:07,923
generation in history,

5
00:00:07,923 --> 00:00:10,307
and yet millions of people with good

6
00:00:10,307 --> 00:00:12,427
jobs still feel broke at the end

7
00:00:12,427 --> 00:00:13,421
of every month.

8
00:00:13,601 --> 00:00:14,390
Not poor.

9
00:00:14,570 --> 00:00:14,977
Broke.

10
00:00:15,157 --> 00:00:16,593
The paycheck comes in,

11
00:00:16,593 --> 00:00:18,616
and a few days later it's gone.

12
00:00:18,796 --> 00:00:20,886
So today, the big question.

13
00:00:21,066 --> 00:00:22,842
Why do most people stay broke?

14
00:00:23,022 --> 00:00:24,342
And more importantly,

15
00:00:24,342 --> 00:00:25,472
how do you escape?

16
00:00:25,822 --> 00:00:28,368
The most common money pattern in America

17
00:00:28,368 --> 00:00:29,259
isn't poverty.

18
00:00:29,439 --> 00:00:31,285
It's the paycheck to paycheck loop.

19
00:00:31,465 --> 00:00:32,550
Money comes in,

20
00:00:32,550 --> 00:00:33,636
money goes out,

21
00:00:33,636 --> 00:00:35,517
and almost nothing sticks.

22
00:00:35,866 --> 00:00:37,747
Here's the uncomfortable truth.

23
00:00:37,927 --> 00:00:40,486
Staying broke is rarely about one big

24
00:00:40,486 --> 00:00:41,039
mistake.

25
00:00:41,219 --> 00:00:42,310
It's about a system.

26
00:00:42,490 --> 00:00:44,847
Small leaks, habits you never chose on

27
00:00:44,847 --> 00:00:46,894
purpose, and a few traps that are

28
00:00:46,894 --> 00:00:49,189
designed by very smart people to keep

29
00:00:49,189 --> 00:00:51,546
your money moving out of your account.

30
00:00:51,726 --> 00:00:53,375
Once you see that system,

31
00:00:53,375 --> 00:00:54,628
you can't unsee it.

32
00:00:54,808 --> 00:00:56,875
And that's where the escape starts.

33
00:00:57,224 --> 00:00:59,024
We're going to look at this from

34
00:00:59,024 --> 00:00:59,755
three angles.

35
00:00:59,935 --> 00:01:01,514
First, the numbers.

36
00:01:01,694 --> 00:01:04,060
What the data actually says about how

37
00:01:04,060 --> 00:01:05,466
Americans earn, spend,

38
00:01:05,466 --> 00:01:06,489
save and borrow.

39
00:01:06,669 --> 00:01:08,515
Second, the psychology.

40
00:01:08,695 --> 00:01:11,067
The ten mental traps that keep smart

41
00:01:11,067 --> 00:01:11,923
people broke.

42
00:01:12,103 --> 00:01:13,763
And third, the playbook.

43
00:01:13,943 --> 00:01:15,992
A step by step escape plan you

44
00:01:15,992 --> 00:01:17,358
can start this week,

45
00:01:17,358 --> 00:01:19,202
even on an ordinary income.

46
00:01:19,552 --> 00:01:20,648
Stay to the end,

47
00:01:20,648 --> 00:01:23,044
because the escape plan is the part

48
00:01:23,044 --> 00:01:24,208
most videos skip.

49
00:01:24,388 --> 00:01:26,060
Let's start with the numbers.

50
00:01:26,409 --> 00:01:28,780
Every number in this video comes from

51
00:01:28,780 --> 00:01:29,742
a named source.

52
00:01:29,922 --> 00:01:33,212
Household finances come from the Federal Reserve,

53
00:01:33,212 --> 00:01:36,099
including its yearly survey on the economic

54
00:01:36,099 --> 00:01:38,383
well being of American households,

55
00:01:38,383 --> 00:01:40,800
and the Survey of Consumer Finances.

56
00:01:40,980 --> 00:01:43,377
Debt totals come from the Federal Reserve

57
00:01:43,377 --> 00:01:44,370
Bank of New York.

58
00:01:44,550 --> 00:01:46,857
Saving rates come from the Bureau of

59
00:01:46,857 --> 00:01:48,010
Economic Analysis.

60
00:01:48,190 --> 00:01:49,840
Where I use an example,

61
00:01:49,840 --> 00:01:51,562
it's simple stated math,

62
00:01:51,562 --> 00:01:52,567
not a promise.

63
00:01:52,747 --> 00:01:54,135
This is education,

64
00:01:54,135 --> 00:01:55,754
not financial advice.

65
00:01:56,103 --> 00:01:56,812
Angle one.

66
00:01:56,992 --> 00:01:57,758
The numbers.

67
00:01:57,938 --> 00:01:59,367
Before we talk about why,

68
00:01:59,367 --> 00:02:01,653
let's look at what's actually happening.

69
00:02:02,003 --> 00:02:03,946
Start with the most famous test in

70
00:02:03,946 --> 00:02:04,918
personal finance.

71
00:02:05,098 --> 00:02:07,677
The Federal Reserve asks adults how they

72
00:02:07,677 --> 00:02:10,063
would pay for a surprise four hundred

73
00:02:10,063 --> 00:02:11,030
dollar expense.

74
00:02:11,210 --> 00:02:12,214
In recent years,

75
00:02:12,214 --> 00:02:14,159
only about six in ten said they

76
00:02:14,159 --> 00:02:16,104
would cover it with cash or its

77
00:02:16,104 --> 00:02:16,795
equivalent.

78
00:02:16,975 --> 00:02:19,376
That means roughly four in ten would

79
00:02:19,376 --> 00:02:20,376
need to borrow,

80
00:02:20,376 --> 00:02:22,977
sell something, or simply couldn't pay.

81
00:02:23,326 --> 00:02:25,775
Think about how small four hundred dollars

82
00:02:25,775 --> 00:02:25,950
is.

83
00:02:26,130 --> 00:02:27,198
A car repair.

84
00:02:27,378 --> 00:02:28,249
A vet bill.

85
00:02:28,429 --> 00:02:29,706
A dental visit.

86
00:02:29,886 --> 00:02:31,340
For millions of households,

87
00:02:31,340 --> 00:02:33,439
an expense like that doesn't just hurt.

88
00:02:33,619 --> 00:02:35,691
It goes straight onto a credit card,

89
00:02:35,691 --> 00:02:37,590
and that's where the loop begins.

90
00:02:37,770 --> 00:02:39,415
The emergency becomes debt,

91
00:02:39,415 --> 00:02:40,999
the debt becomes interest,

92
00:02:40,999 --> 00:02:43,375
and the interest becomes a monthly bill

93
00:02:43,375 --> 00:02:45,386
that eats into the next paycheck.

94
00:02:45,735 --> 00:02:46,885
Now the debt side.

95
00:02:47,065 --> 00:02:49,644
According to the Federal Reserve Bank of

96
00:02:49,644 --> 00:02:52,030
New York, Americans owe more than one

97
00:02:52,030 --> 00:02:54,159
trillion dollars on credit cards.

98
00:02:54,339 --> 00:02:56,960
That's more than one thousand billion dollars,

99
00:02:56,960 --> 00:02:59,012
much of it charged interest at rates

100
00:02:59,012 --> 00:03:00,608
above twenty percent a year.

101
00:03:00,957 --> 00:03:03,121
Here's what that rate means in real

102
00:03:03,121 --> 00:03:03,430
life.

103
00:03:03,610 --> 00:03:05,763
If you carry a five thousand dollar

104
00:03:05,763 --> 00:03:07,608
balance at twenty two percent,

105
00:03:07,608 --> 00:03:10,068
you pay roughly eleven hundred dollars a

106
00:03:10,068 --> 00:03:11,482
year in interest alone.

107
00:03:11,662 --> 00:03:14,077
That's eleven hundred dollars that buys nothing.

108
00:03:14,257 --> 00:03:16,207
No groceries, no fun,

109
00:03:16,207 --> 00:03:17,322
no progress.

110
00:03:17,502 --> 00:03:19,491
It's the price of having spent money

111
00:03:19,491 --> 00:03:20,486
before you had it.

112
00:03:20,666 --> 00:03:23,110
And because the minimum payment is so

113
00:03:23,110 --> 00:03:25,819
small, many people carry that balance for

114
00:03:25,819 --> 00:03:26,215
years.

115
00:03:26,565 --> 00:03:28,420
Let's look at one of those minimum

116
00:03:28,420 --> 00:03:28,911
payments.

117
00:03:29,091 --> 00:03:32,024
Five thousand dollars at twenty two percent,

118
00:03:32,024 --> 00:03:34,624
paying only a small minimum each month,

119
00:03:34,624 --> 00:03:36,624
can take well over a decade to

120
00:03:36,624 --> 00:03:39,090
clear, and the total interest can end

121
00:03:39,090 --> 00:03:41,490
up larger than the original balance.

122
00:03:41,670 --> 00:03:43,859
Paying a fixed two hundred dollars a

123
00:03:43,859 --> 00:03:46,170
month instead clears it in about three

124
00:03:46,170 --> 00:03:46,535
years.

125
00:03:46,884 --> 00:03:48,463
Now the savings side.

126
00:03:48,643 --> 00:03:51,816
The Bureau of Economic Analysis tracks something

127
00:03:51,816 --> 00:03:53,931
called the personal saving rate,

128
00:03:53,931 --> 00:03:56,179
the share of after tax income that

129
00:03:56,179 --> 00:03:57,699
households don't spend.

130
00:03:57,879 --> 00:03:59,822
In recent years it has often sat

131
00:03:59,822 --> 00:04:02,128
somewhere around four to five percent.

132
00:04:02,308 --> 00:04:03,224
In other words,

133
00:04:03,224 --> 00:04:05,726
out of every hundred dollars that reaches

134
00:04:05,726 --> 00:04:07,069
the average household,

135
00:04:07,069 --> 00:04:08,961
roughly ninety five gets spent.

136
00:04:09,310 --> 00:04:10,506
And that's the average.

137
00:04:10,686 --> 00:04:12,091
Averages hide a lot.

138
00:04:12,271 --> 00:04:14,319
Some households save a big share of

139
00:04:14,319 --> 00:04:15,197
what they earn.

140
00:04:15,377 --> 00:04:17,000
Many save nothing at all,

141
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:19,271
and some spend more than they make,

142
00:04:19,271 --> 00:04:21,089
filling the gap with credit.

143
00:04:21,269 --> 00:04:23,669
When you hear that Americans save five

144
00:04:23,669 --> 00:04:25,942
percent, picture a crowd where a few

145
00:04:25,942 --> 00:04:27,079
people save a lot,

146
00:04:27,079 --> 00:04:29,036
and a lot of people are running

147
00:04:29,036 --> 00:04:29,605
on empty.

148
00:04:29,954 --> 00:04:31,975
So where does the money actually go?

149
00:04:32,155 --> 00:04:34,804
The Bureau of Labor Statistics runs a

150
00:04:34,804 --> 00:04:39,028
Consumer Expenditure Survey that tracks household spending.

151
00:04:39,208 --> 00:04:40,264
Year after year,

152
00:04:40,264 --> 00:04:42,575
the same three categories dominate.

153
00:04:42,755 --> 00:04:44,961
Housing takes roughly a third of the

154
00:04:44,961 --> 00:04:46,493
typical household budget.

155
00:04:46,673 --> 00:04:48,942
Transportation, mostly cars,

156
00:04:48,942 --> 00:04:50,806
takes around one sixth.

157
00:04:50,986 --> 00:04:53,554
And food, at home and eating out,

158
00:04:53,554 --> 00:04:55,421
takes around one eighth.

159
00:04:55,771 --> 00:04:58,523
Housing, transportation and food.

160
00:04:58,703 --> 00:05:00,994
Together, they make up around six of

161
00:05:00,994 --> 00:05:03,858
every ten dollars a typical household spends.

162
00:05:04,038 --> 00:05:06,109
That's where the big wins are hiding,

163
00:05:06,109 --> 00:05:07,509
not in skipping a coffee.

164
00:05:07,859 --> 00:05:10,819
This matters because most money advice focuses

165
00:05:10,819 --> 00:05:11,784
on tiny things.

166
00:05:11,964 --> 00:05:13,264
Skip the latte.

167
00:05:13,444 --> 00:05:14,349
Bring lunch.

168
00:05:14,529 --> 00:05:15,673
Those habits help,

169
00:05:15,673 --> 00:05:16,817
but they're small.

170
00:05:16,997 --> 00:05:18,436
One decision on rent,

171
00:05:18,436 --> 00:05:19,943
one decision on a car,

172
00:05:19,943 --> 00:05:22,204
can be worth more than a thousand

173
00:05:22,204 --> 00:05:23,301
skipped coffees.

174
00:05:23,481 --> 00:05:25,669
A car payment of six hundred dollars

175
00:05:25,669 --> 00:05:27,919
versus three hundred dollars is a gap

176
00:05:27,919 --> 00:05:30,290
of three thousand six hundred dollars a

177
00:05:30,290 --> 00:05:31,323
year, every year,

178
00:05:31,323 --> 00:05:32,479
for the whole loan.

179
00:05:32,659 --> 00:05:34,596
If you want to escape faster,

180
00:05:34,596 --> 00:05:36,467
look at the big three first.

181
00:05:36,816 --> 00:05:39,243
Here's another number that surprises people.

182
00:05:39,423 --> 00:05:42,352
The Federal Reserve's Survey of Consumer Finances

183
00:05:42,352 --> 00:05:44,683
found the median net worth for families

184
00:05:44,683 --> 00:05:47,014
headed by someone under thirty five was

185
00:05:47,014 --> 00:05:49,644
about thirty nine thousand dollars in twenty

186
00:05:49,644 --> 00:05:50,301
twenty two.

187
00:05:50,481 --> 00:05:51,423
Half had more,

188
00:05:51,423 --> 00:05:52,365
half had less,

189
00:05:52,365 --> 00:05:54,922
and that includes things like cars and

190
00:05:54,922 --> 00:05:55,729
home equity.

191
00:05:56,079 --> 00:05:57,891
So what does the data tell us?

192
00:05:58,071 --> 00:05:59,807
It tells us that being broke is

193
00:05:59,807 --> 00:06:01,656
not rare, and it's not a personal

194
00:06:01,656 --> 00:06:04,177
failing that only happens to careless people.

195
00:06:04,357 --> 00:06:06,699
It's the normal outcome of a normal

196
00:06:06,699 --> 00:06:07,167
system.

197
00:06:07,347 --> 00:06:08,449
Income goes up,

198
00:06:08,449 --> 00:06:09,919
spending follows it,

199
00:06:09,919 --> 00:06:12,270
and easy credit fills every gap.

200
00:06:12,450 --> 00:06:14,257
If you do what everyone else does,

201
00:06:14,257 --> 00:06:16,118
you'll get what everyone else gets.

202
00:06:16,468 --> 00:06:18,303
But there's a second message in the

203
00:06:18,303 --> 00:06:19,928
data, and it's the hopeful one.

204
00:06:20,108 --> 00:06:22,533
The difference between the households who build

205
00:06:22,533 --> 00:06:24,494
wealth and the households who don't is

206
00:06:24,494 --> 00:06:25,681
often not their income.

207
00:06:25,861 --> 00:06:27,626
Plenty of high earners are broke.

208
00:06:27,806 --> 00:06:30,711
Plenty of ordinary earners quietly build a

209
00:06:30,711 --> 00:06:32,717
cushion and then investments.

210
00:06:32,897 --> 00:06:34,960
The gap is mostly in what happens

211
00:06:34,960 --> 00:06:36,461
after the money arrives.

212
00:06:36,641 --> 00:06:38,348
And that's something you can change.

213
00:06:38,697 --> 00:06:40,555
Look at what that gap can become.

214
00:06:40,735 --> 00:06:43,726
Suppose you redirect just three hundred dollars

215
00:06:43,726 --> 00:06:45,635
a month that used to leak out,

216
00:06:45,635 --> 00:06:47,671
and invest it at a seven percent

217
00:06:47,671 --> 00:06:49,071
average yearly return.

218
00:06:49,251 --> 00:06:50,408
After twenty years,

219
00:06:50,408 --> 00:06:52,723
you'd have put in seventy two thousand

220
00:06:52,723 --> 00:06:53,210
dollars.

221
00:06:53,390 --> 00:06:55,352
Here's roughly what it could grow to.

222
00:06:55,702 --> 00:06:57,261
That's an illustration,

223
00:06:57,261 --> 00:06:58,210
not a promise.

224
00:06:58,390 --> 00:06:59,741
Markets go up and down,

225
00:06:59,741 --> 00:07:01,385
and some years are negative.

226
00:07:01,565 --> 00:07:02,947
But the point is clear.

227
00:07:03,127 --> 00:07:05,125
Three hundred dollars a month is not

228
00:07:05,125 --> 00:07:05,902
a lottery win.

229
00:07:06,082 --> 00:07:07,138
It's a car payment.

230
00:07:07,318 --> 00:07:09,931
A few subscriptions and takeout nights.

231
00:07:10,111 --> 00:07:11,947
The money that keeps people broke is

232
00:07:11,947 --> 00:07:13,783
often the same money that could make

233
00:07:13,783 --> 00:07:14,650
them comfortable.

234
00:07:15,000 --> 00:07:17,097
There's one more set of numbers that

235
00:07:17,097 --> 00:07:18,379
rarely makes the news.

236
00:07:18,559 --> 00:07:20,266
Being broke is expensive.

237
00:07:20,446 --> 00:07:21,688
Not in a poetic way.

238
00:07:21,868 --> 00:07:23,215
In actual dollars.

239
00:07:23,395 --> 00:07:24,863
When you have no cushion,

240
00:07:24,863 --> 00:07:26,918
you pay fees and prices that people

241
00:07:26,918 --> 00:07:28,445
with savings simply avoid.

242
00:07:28,625 --> 00:07:29,844
Let me show you a few.

243
00:07:30,193 --> 00:07:32,869
Overdraft and late fees are the obvious

244
00:07:32,869 --> 00:07:33,212
ones.

245
00:07:33,392 --> 00:07:36,249
One overdraft can cost around thirty dollars

246
00:07:36,249 --> 00:07:37,158
at many banks,

247
00:07:37,158 --> 00:07:39,170
even if the purchase was a five

248
00:07:39,170 --> 00:07:40,079
dollar coffee.

249
00:07:40,259 --> 00:07:42,401
A late card payment can add a

250
00:07:42,401 --> 00:07:45,356
fee and sometimes a higher penalty rate.

251
00:07:45,536 --> 00:07:47,672
People with a cushion rarely pay these.

252
00:07:47,852 --> 00:07:50,161
People without one can pay them every

253
00:07:50,161 --> 00:07:50,848
few months.

254
00:07:51,197 --> 00:07:52,300
Then there's credit.

255
00:07:52,480 --> 00:07:55,257
A lower credit score usually means higher

256
00:07:55,257 --> 00:07:56,747
interest on car loans,

257
00:07:56,747 --> 00:07:59,727
higher deposits on apartments and utilities,

258
00:07:59,727 --> 00:08:01,014
and in many states,

259
00:08:01,014 --> 00:08:03,045
higher car insurance premiums.

260
00:08:03,225 --> 00:08:05,010
Two people can buy the same car

261
00:08:05,010 --> 00:08:06,737
at the same price and pay very

262
00:08:06,737 --> 00:08:08,924
different amounts over the life of the

263
00:08:08,924 --> 00:08:11,399
loan, just because of their credit history.

264
00:08:11,748 --> 00:08:13,861
And there's the cost of buying small.

265
00:08:14,041 --> 00:08:15,293
When money is tight,

266
00:08:15,293 --> 00:08:17,358
you buy the small pack instead of

267
00:08:17,358 --> 00:08:18,234
the bulk pack,

268
00:08:18,234 --> 00:08:20,237
the cheap shoes that wear out in

269
00:08:20,237 --> 00:08:22,177
a year instead of the good pair

270
00:08:22,177 --> 00:08:23,178
that lasts five.

271
00:08:23,358 --> 00:08:25,344
Each choice makes sense in the moment.

272
00:08:25,524 --> 00:08:26,319
Over a year,

273
00:08:26,319 --> 00:08:27,114
they add up.

274
00:08:27,294 --> 00:08:29,242
This is why the first cushion matters

275
00:08:29,242 --> 00:08:29,663
so much.

276
00:08:29,843 --> 00:08:31,166
It's not just safety.

277
00:08:31,346 --> 00:08:33,578
It's the ticket out of the expensive

278
00:08:33,578 --> 00:08:34,446
way of living.

279
00:08:34,796 --> 00:08:35,688
Add it all up,

280
00:08:35,688 --> 00:08:38,808
and many households without savings lose hundreds

281
00:08:38,808 --> 00:08:41,037
or even thousands of dollars a year

282
00:08:41,037 --> 00:08:43,330
to fees, interest and higher prices.

283
00:08:43,510 --> 00:08:45,301
That money is the hidden tax on

284
00:08:45,301 --> 00:08:45,994
being broke.

285
00:08:46,174 --> 00:08:47,293
And the good news is,

286
00:08:47,293 --> 00:08:49,052
it's also one of the first things

287
00:08:49,052 --> 00:08:50,598
you get back when you escape.

288
00:08:50,947 --> 00:08:52,677
So the numbers show the what.

289
00:08:52,857 --> 00:08:54,285
Now let's get to the why.

290
00:08:54,465 --> 00:08:56,417
Because if the math were all it

291
00:08:56,417 --> 00:08:58,180
took, nobody would be broke.

292
00:08:58,530 --> 00:08:59,517
Angle two.

293
00:08:59,697 --> 00:09:00,858
The psychology.

294
00:09:01,038 --> 00:09:03,204
These are the ten mental traps that

295
00:09:03,204 --> 00:09:05,555
keep smart, hard working people broke.

296
00:09:05,735 --> 00:09:07,418
See how many you recognize.

297
00:09:07,767 --> 00:09:08,859
Reason number one.

298
00:09:09,039 --> 00:09:10,734
Scarcity tunnels your vision.

299
00:09:11,083 --> 00:09:15,029
Researchers Sendhil Mullainathan and Eldar Shafir wrote

300
00:09:15,029 --> 00:09:16,679
a book called Scarcity.

301
00:09:16,859 --> 00:09:18,763
Their core idea is simple.

302
00:09:18,943 --> 00:09:20,436
When you're short on money,

303
00:09:20,436 --> 00:09:22,426
your mind focuses hard on the urgent

304
00:09:22,426 --> 00:09:23,753
problem in front of you,

305
00:09:23,753 --> 00:09:24,969
like this week's rent.

306
00:09:25,149 --> 00:09:26,860
That focus helps in the moment,

307
00:09:26,860 --> 00:09:28,737
but it pushes everything else out.

308
00:09:28,917 --> 00:09:30,456
Long term planning,

309
00:09:30,456 --> 00:09:32,481
saving, comparing prices.

310
00:09:32,661 --> 00:09:33,764
They called it tunneling.

311
00:09:34,113 --> 00:09:35,553
Here's what that looks like.

312
00:09:35,733 --> 00:09:37,213
You're short this month,

313
00:09:37,213 --> 00:09:39,125
so you put the electric bill on

314
00:09:39,125 --> 00:09:40,482
a card to get through.

315
00:09:40,662 --> 00:09:42,833
It solves today's problem perfectly.

316
00:09:43,013 --> 00:09:45,203
But it creates next month's problem,

317
00:09:45,203 --> 00:09:46,054
with interest.

318
00:09:46,234 --> 00:09:47,922
When you're inside the tunnel,

319
00:09:47,922 --> 00:09:49,497
that trade looks reasonable.

320
00:09:49,677 --> 00:09:51,488
From outside, it's a trap.

321
00:09:51,668 --> 00:09:54,051
Try this: make your money decisions on

322
00:09:54,051 --> 00:09:54,741
a calm day,

323
00:09:54,741 --> 00:09:55,557
once a month,

324
00:09:55,557 --> 00:09:57,438
not in the middle of a crisis.

325
00:09:57,788 --> 00:09:58,949
Reason number two.

326
00:09:59,129 --> 00:10:00,290
Present bias.

327
00:10:00,470 --> 00:10:02,003
Today always wins.

328
00:10:02,352 --> 00:10:04,956
Economists use the term present bias for

329
00:10:04,956 --> 00:10:06,323
a simple human habit.

330
00:10:06,503 --> 00:10:08,514
We value a reward today much more

331
00:10:08,514 --> 00:10:10,160
than a bigger reward later.

332
00:10:10,340 --> 00:10:12,890
Fifty dollars now feels better than seventy

333
00:10:12,890 --> 00:10:13,958
dollars next year,

334
00:10:13,958 --> 00:10:16,092
even though the second is the better

335
00:10:16,092 --> 00:10:16,389
deal.

336
00:10:16,569 --> 00:10:18,795
Saving asks you to give up something

337
00:10:18,795 --> 00:10:21,268
real today for something abstract in the

338
00:10:21,268 --> 00:10:21,701
future.

339
00:10:21,881 --> 00:10:24,191
Your brain is wired to resist that.

340
00:10:24,540 --> 00:10:27,246
This is why willpower alone fails.

341
00:10:27,426 --> 00:10:28,320
Every single day,

342
00:10:28,320 --> 00:10:30,423
the present makes a better argument than

343
00:10:30,423 --> 00:10:31,001
the future.

344
00:10:31,181 --> 00:10:32,795
The fix isn't to try harder.

345
00:10:32,975 --> 00:10:34,821
It's to remove the daily decision.

346
00:10:35,001 --> 00:10:37,618
When saving happens automatically on payday,

347
00:10:37,618 --> 00:10:39,401
the present never gets a vote.

348
00:10:39,581 --> 00:10:42,111
Try this: set an automatic transfer to

349
00:10:42,111 --> 00:10:44,708
savings for the day after your paycheck

350
00:10:44,708 --> 00:10:45,108
lands.

351
00:10:45,288 --> 00:10:46,878
Start with any amount.

352
00:10:47,228 --> 00:10:48,471
Reason number three.

353
00:10:48,651 --> 00:10:49,963
Comparison spending.

354
00:10:50,312 --> 00:10:52,964
People have always compared themselves with their

355
00:10:52,964 --> 00:10:53,505
neighbors.

356
00:10:53,685 --> 00:10:55,792
The difference now is that your neighbors

357
00:10:55,792 --> 00:10:57,180
are everyone on your phone.

358
00:10:57,360 --> 00:10:58,660
You see vacations,

359
00:10:58,660 --> 00:11:01,982
new cars, renovated kitchens and designer bags

360
00:11:01,982 --> 00:11:02,921
all day long.

361
00:11:03,101 --> 00:11:05,144
You don't see the loans behind them.

362
00:11:05,324 --> 00:11:08,211
And because what's normal keeps shifting upward,

363
00:11:08,211 --> 00:11:10,917
ordinary spending starts to feel like falling

364
00:11:10,917 --> 00:11:11,338
behind.

365
00:11:11,688 --> 00:11:12,757
A useful reminder:

366
00:11:12,757 --> 00:11:15,666
you're comparing your full financial picture with

367
00:11:15,666 --> 00:11:17,447
someone else's highlight reel.

368
00:11:17,627 --> 00:11:19,183
The car is visible.

369
00:11:19,363 --> 00:11:20,744
The car payment isn't.

370
00:11:20,924 --> 00:11:22,248
The trip is visible.

371
00:11:22,428 --> 00:11:24,274
The credit card balance isn't.

372
00:11:24,454 --> 00:11:26,186
Try this: for one week,

373
00:11:26,186 --> 00:11:29,124
before any purchase over fifty dollars,

374
00:11:29,124 --> 00:11:31,083
ask yourself one question.

375
00:11:31,263 --> 00:11:33,017
Would I still buy this if nobody

376
00:11:33,017 --> 00:11:34,003
could ever see it?

377
00:11:34,353 --> 00:11:35,572
Reason number four.

378
00:11:35,752 --> 00:11:37,146
Lifestyle creep.

379
00:11:37,495 --> 00:11:40,108
Lifestyle creep is what happens when your

380
00:11:40,108 --> 00:11:42,848
spending rises every time your income does.

381
00:11:43,028 --> 00:11:44,127
A raise arrives,

382
00:11:44,127 --> 00:11:46,325
and soon the apartment is nicer,

383
00:11:46,325 --> 00:11:47,493
the car is newer,

384
00:11:47,493 --> 00:11:49,622
and dinners out become a habit.

385
00:11:49,802 --> 00:11:51,927
None of these choices feels big.

386
00:11:52,107 --> 00:11:54,393
But together they absorb the whole raise,

387
00:11:54,393 --> 00:11:55,954
and you end up with the same

388
00:11:55,954 --> 00:11:57,459
savings at a higher income.

389
00:11:57,808 --> 00:11:59,213
Here's the sneaky part.

390
00:11:59,393 --> 00:12:02,076
Upgrades are easy to make and painful

391
00:12:02,076 --> 00:12:02,656
to undo.

392
00:12:02,836 --> 00:12:05,285
Once you're used to a better phone,

393
00:12:05,285 --> 00:12:08,155
a bigger place or a premium subscription,

394
00:12:08,155 --> 00:12:10,185
going back feels like a loss.

395
00:12:10,365 --> 00:12:13,267
Psychologists call this the hedonic treadmill.

396
00:12:13,447 --> 00:12:15,909
The fix is to decide in advance.

397
00:12:16,089 --> 00:12:18,099
Try this: next time you get a

398
00:12:18,099 --> 00:12:20,316
raise, send at least half of the

399
00:12:20,316 --> 00:12:23,297
increase to savings or investing before you

400
00:12:23,297 --> 00:12:25,307
ever get used to spending it.

401
00:12:25,656 --> 00:12:26,992
Reason number five.

402
00:12:27,172 --> 00:12:28,286
Mental accounting.

403
00:12:28,636 --> 00:12:30,325
Economist Richard Thaler,

404
00:12:30,325 --> 00:12:32,216
who later won a Nobel prize,

405
00:12:32,216 --> 00:12:35,120
described how people sort money into mental

406
00:12:35,120 --> 00:12:35,660
buckets.

407
00:12:35,840 --> 00:12:37,861
Tax refunds feel like bonus money.

408
00:12:38,041 --> 00:12:40,293
Cash feels more real than a card.

409
00:12:40,473 --> 00:12:42,617
A twenty dollar bill found in a

410
00:12:42,617 --> 00:12:43,724
coat feels free.

411
00:12:43,904 --> 00:12:45,459
But a dollar is a dollar.

412
00:12:45,639 --> 00:12:47,539
Treating some money as play money is

413
00:12:47,539 --> 00:12:49,122
one of the easiest ways for it

414
00:12:49,122 --> 00:12:49,807
to disappear.

415
00:12:50,157 --> 00:12:51,829
Think about the tax refund.

416
00:12:52,009 --> 00:12:54,008
Many people treat it like a gift

417
00:12:54,008 --> 00:12:55,881
and spend it on something fun,

418
00:12:55,881 --> 00:12:58,317
while carrying a credit card balance at

419
00:12:58,317 --> 00:12:59,254
twenty percent.

420
00:12:59,434 --> 00:13:01,318
Paying down the card would be worth

421
00:13:01,318 --> 00:13:01,802
far more.

422
00:13:01,982 --> 00:13:04,506
Try this: give every windfall a plan

423
00:13:04,506 --> 00:13:05,767
before it arrives.

424
00:13:05,947 --> 00:13:07,584
A simple split works.

425
00:13:07,764 --> 00:13:10,063
For example, most of it to debt

426
00:13:10,063 --> 00:13:11,843
or savings, and a small,

427
00:13:11,843 --> 00:13:13,697
guilt free slice for fun.

428
00:13:14,046 --> 00:13:15,927
We're halfway through the traps.

429
00:13:16,107 --> 00:13:17,734
The next one is the one I

430
00:13:17,734 --> 00:13:18,710
see most often,

431
00:13:18,710 --> 00:13:20,728
and it's hiding in plain sight.

432
00:13:21,078 --> 00:13:22,576
Reason number six.

433
00:13:22,756 --> 00:13:23,847
The ostrich effect.

434
00:13:24,197 --> 00:13:26,812
Researchers have found that people check their

435
00:13:26,812 --> 00:13:29,085
accounts more often when markets are up,

436
00:13:29,085 --> 00:13:31,245
and avoid looking when things are bad.

437
00:13:31,425 --> 00:13:33,265
It's called the ostrich effect,

438
00:13:33,265 --> 00:13:35,639
after the myth that ostriches bury their

439
00:13:35,639 --> 00:13:36,707
heads in the sand.

440
00:13:36,887 --> 00:13:38,109
When money is tight,

441
00:13:38,109 --> 00:13:40,430
opening the banking app feels painful,

442
00:13:40,430 --> 00:13:42,019
so people stop opening it.

443
00:13:42,199 --> 00:13:43,751
And what you don't look at,

444
00:13:43,751 --> 00:13:44,556
you can't fix.

445
00:13:44,905 --> 00:13:46,902
Not looking has real costs.

446
00:13:47,082 --> 00:13:49,625
Missed due dates lead to late fees.

447
00:13:49,805 --> 00:13:52,313
Forgotten subscriptions keep renewing.

448
00:13:52,493 --> 00:13:54,222
Interest quietly grows.

449
00:13:54,402 --> 00:13:55,947
The irony is that the anxiety of

450
00:13:55,947 --> 00:13:57,732
not knowing is usually worse than the

451
00:13:57,732 --> 00:13:58,408
number itself.

452
00:13:58,588 --> 00:14:01,452
Try this: schedule a fifteen minute money

453
00:14:01,452 --> 00:14:02,779
check every Sunday.

454
00:14:02,959 --> 00:14:05,061
Same time, same coffee.

455
00:14:05,241 --> 00:14:06,076
Just look.

456
00:14:06,256 --> 00:14:07,081
No judgment.

457
00:14:07,430 --> 00:14:08,696
Reason number seven.

458
00:14:08,876 --> 00:14:10,594
Credit makes spending painless.

459
00:14:10,944 --> 00:14:13,845
Researchers have long observed that paying with

460
00:14:13,845 --> 00:14:16,067
a card often feels less painful than

461
00:14:16,067 --> 00:14:17,116
paying with cash,

462
00:14:17,116 --> 00:14:19,214
and people tend to spend more when

463
00:14:19,214 --> 00:14:20,325
the pain is lower.

464
00:14:20,505 --> 00:14:21,278
Tap to pay,

465
00:14:21,278 --> 00:14:22,613
one click checkout,

466
00:14:22,613 --> 00:14:25,002
saved cards, and buy now pay later

467
00:14:25,002 --> 00:14:27,672
all remove the tiny moment of friction

468
00:14:27,672 --> 00:14:29,991
that used to make us think twice.

469
00:14:30,340 --> 00:14:31,629
Credit isn't evil.

470
00:14:31,809 --> 00:14:33,527
Used well, it's a tool.

471
00:14:33,707 --> 00:14:35,872
But when spending becomes painless,

472
00:14:35,872 --> 00:14:37,481
it also becomes invisible.

473
00:14:37,661 --> 00:14:39,405
And the bill arrives later,

474
00:14:39,405 --> 00:14:41,213
when the excitement is gone.

475
00:14:41,393 --> 00:14:43,755
Try this: remove saved cards from your

476
00:14:43,755 --> 00:14:45,434
shopping apps and browsers.

477
00:14:45,614 --> 00:14:47,675
That extra minute of typing your card

478
00:14:47,675 --> 00:14:49,236
number back in is one of the

479
00:14:49,236 --> 00:14:50,908
cheapest money tools there is.

480
00:14:51,257 --> 00:14:52,372
Reason number eight.

481
00:14:52,552 --> 00:14:53,841
Optimism bias.

482
00:14:54,190 --> 00:14:56,147
Most people believe next month will be

483
00:14:56,147 --> 00:14:57,383
cheaper than this month.

484
00:14:57,563 --> 00:14:59,407
This month had a birthday,

485
00:14:59,407 --> 00:15:00,329
a car repair,

486
00:15:00,329 --> 00:15:00,826
a trip.

487
00:15:01,006 --> 00:15:02,399
Next month will be normal.

488
00:15:02,579 --> 00:15:04,123
But every month has something.

489
00:15:04,303 --> 00:15:07,306
Psychologists call the habit of expecting things

490
00:15:07,306 --> 00:15:09,809
to go better than average optimism bias,

491
00:15:09,809 --> 00:15:11,498
and in money it shows up as

492
00:15:11,498 --> 00:15:14,438
budgets that never include the irregular stuff.

493
00:15:14,788 --> 00:15:16,214
The fix is to plan for the

494
00:15:16,214 --> 00:15:17,970
irregular as if it were regular.

495
00:15:18,150 --> 00:15:20,085
Car maintenance, gifts,

496
00:15:20,085 --> 00:15:23,200
annual subscriptions, medical copays.

497
00:15:23,380 --> 00:15:25,071
Add up what you spent on these

498
00:15:25,071 --> 00:15:26,875
over the last year and divide by

499
00:15:26,875 --> 00:15:27,269
twelve.

500
00:15:27,449 --> 00:15:29,319
That's a real monthly expense,

501
00:15:29,319 --> 00:15:31,501
even if it doesn't show up monthly.

502
00:15:31,681 --> 00:15:34,338
Try this: open a separate savings bucket

503
00:15:34,338 --> 00:15:37,326
called irregular, and put that monthly amount

504
00:15:37,326 --> 00:15:37,858
into it.

505
00:15:38,207 --> 00:15:39,554
Reason number nine.

506
00:15:39,734 --> 00:15:40,535
Identity.

507
00:15:40,885 --> 00:15:43,730
Many people carry an unspoken belief about

508
00:15:43,730 --> 00:15:44,136
money.

509
00:15:44,316 --> 00:15:46,174
People like me aren't good with money.

510
00:15:46,354 --> 00:15:47,724
Rich people are different.

511
00:15:47,904 --> 00:15:49,622
Investing is for experts.

512
00:15:49,802 --> 00:15:52,336
Those beliefs often come from childhood,

513
00:15:52,336 --> 00:15:54,489
from how money was talked about at

514
00:15:54,489 --> 00:15:54,806
home.

515
00:15:54,986 --> 00:15:57,301
And they quietly decide what you even

516
00:15:57,301 --> 00:15:57,552
try.

517
00:15:57,901 --> 00:15:58,784
Here's the thing.

518
00:15:58,964 --> 00:16:01,541
Being good with money isn't a personality.

519
00:16:01,721 --> 00:16:03,428
It's a short list of habits.

520
00:16:03,608 --> 00:16:04,908
Spend less than you earn.

521
00:16:05,088 --> 00:16:05,889
Keep a cushion.

522
00:16:06,069 --> 00:16:07,695
Avoid high interest debt.

523
00:16:07,875 --> 00:16:09,999
Invest the difference for a long time.

524
00:16:10,179 --> 00:16:11,514
Anyone can learn those.

525
00:16:11,694 --> 00:16:13,863
Try this: replace the sentence I'm bad

526
00:16:13,863 --> 00:16:16,031
with money with I'm learning to manage

527
00:16:16,031 --> 00:16:16,373
money.

528
00:16:16,553 --> 00:16:17,749
It sounds small.

529
00:16:17,929 --> 00:16:20,100
It changes what you're willing to try.

530
00:16:20,450 --> 00:16:21,716
Reason number ten.

531
00:16:21,896 --> 00:16:23,521
Waiting until you earn more.

532
00:16:23,871 --> 00:16:26,047
This is maybe the most common trap

533
00:16:26,047 --> 00:16:26,495
of all.

534
00:16:26,675 --> 00:16:28,241
I'll start saving when I get the

535
00:16:28,241 --> 00:16:28,730
promotion.

536
00:16:28,910 --> 00:16:30,524
When the loan is paid.

537
00:16:30,704 --> 00:16:32,178
When things calm down.

538
00:16:32,358 --> 00:16:33,532
But as we saw,

539
00:16:33,532 --> 00:16:35,041
when income rises,

540
00:16:35,041 --> 00:16:37,304
spending usually rises too.

541
00:16:37,484 --> 00:16:39,912
People who can't save on fifty thousand

542
00:16:39,912 --> 00:16:42,029
dollars a year often can't save on

543
00:16:42,029 --> 00:16:43,460
eighty thousand either,

544
00:16:43,460 --> 00:16:44,581
because the habit,

545
00:16:44,581 --> 00:16:45,514
not the income,

546
00:16:45,514 --> 00:16:46,261
was missing.

547
00:16:46,611 --> 00:16:48,538
The habit has to come first,

548
00:16:48,538 --> 00:16:50,396
even if the number is tiny.

549
00:16:50,576 --> 00:16:52,998
Saving twenty five dollars a week doesn't

550
00:16:52,998 --> 00:16:54,593
change your net worth much.

551
00:16:54,773 --> 00:16:55,783
It changes you.

552
00:16:55,963 --> 00:16:57,360
It proves you can do it,

553
00:16:57,360 --> 00:16:59,338
and it builds the system that will

554
00:16:59,338 --> 00:17:00,967
handle bigger amounts later.

555
00:17:01,147 --> 00:17:03,603
Try this: start with one percent of

556
00:17:03,603 --> 00:17:04,444
your income.

557
00:17:04,624 --> 00:17:06,041
Next month, two.

558
00:17:06,221 --> 00:17:07,833
Raise it until you feel it,

559
00:17:07,833 --> 00:17:09,147
then stop for a while.

560
00:17:09,496 --> 00:17:11,209
Before the escape plan,

561
00:17:11,209 --> 00:17:12,550
a quick self test.

562
00:17:12,730 --> 00:17:15,040
Answer honestly, just for yourself.

563
00:17:15,220 --> 00:17:17,159
Count how many are a yes.

564
00:17:17,509 --> 00:17:17,718
One.

565
00:17:17,898 --> 00:17:19,847
Is your checking account close to zero

566
00:17:19,847 --> 00:17:20,975
the day before payday?

567
00:17:21,155 --> 00:17:21,376
Two.

568
00:17:21,556 --> 00:17:23,507
Do you carry a credit card balance

569
00:17:23,507 --> 00:17:24,655
from month to month?

570
00:17:24,835 --> 00:17:25,358
Three.

571
00:17:25,538 --> 00:17:27,586
If a five hundred dollar bill arrived

572
00:17:27,586 --> 00:17:29,358
tomorrow, would it go on a card?

573
00:17:29,538 --> 00:17:30,014
Four.

574
00:17:30,194 --> 00:17:32,153
Do you avoid opening your banking app

575
00:17:32,153 --> 00:17:33,212
when money is tight?

576
00:17:33,562 --> 00:17:34,096
Five.

577
00:17:34,276 --> 00:17:35,896
Has your income gone up in the

578
00:17:35,896 --> 00:17:38,108
last few years without your savings going

579
00:17:38,108 --> 00:17:38,270
up?

580
00:17:38,450 --> 00:17:38,950
Six.

581
00:17:39,130 --> 00:17:41,255
Do you have subscriptions you haven't used

582
00:17:41,255 --> 00:17:41,811
in a month?

583
00:17:41,991 --> 00:17:42,282
Seven.

584
00:17:42,462 --> 00:17:44,263
Do you say you'll start saving when

585
00:17:44,263 --> 00:17:45,190
something changes?

586
00:17:45,370 --> 00:17:45,834
And eight.

587
00:17:46,014 --> 00:17:46,987
Do you not know,

588
00:17:46,987 --> 00:17:49,114
roughly, what you spent last month?

589
00:17:49,464 --> 00:17:51,133
If you said yes to zero or

590
00:17:51,133 --> 00:17:53,637
one, you're probably already out of the

591
00:17:53,637 --> 00:17:53,957
loop.

592
00:17:54,137 --> 00:17:54,877
Two to four,

593
00:17:54,877 --> 00:17:56,047
you're on the edge,

594
00:17:56,047 --> 00:17:57,834
and a few changes will make a

595
00:17:57,834 --> 00:17:58,758
big difference.

596
00:17:58,938 --> 00:17:59,795
Five or more,

597
00:17:59,795 --> 00:18:00,652
you're in it,

598
00:18:00,652 --> 00:18:02,630
like millions of other people.

599
00:18:02,810 --> 00:18:03,925
That's not a judgment.

600
00:18:04,105 --> 00:18:05,335
It's a starting point.

601
00:18:05,515 --> 00:18:07,652
And every one of those questions maps

602
00:18:07,652 --> 00:18:09,500
to a step in the playbook coming

603
00:18:09,500 --> 00:18:09,962
up next.

604
00:18:10,312 --> 00:18:11,206
Quick pause.

605
00:18:11,386 --> 00:18:13,018
If this is helping you see your

606
00:18:13,018 --> 00:18:15,334
money differently, subscribe to the channel.

607
00:18:15,514 --> 00:18:17,530
It's free, and it helps me keep

608
00:18:17,530 --> 00:18:18,701
making these long,

609
00:18:18,701 --> 00:18:20,262
detailed videos for you.

610
00:18:20,442 --> 00:18:23,368
Now, the part that actually changes things.

611
00:18:23,548 --> 00:18:24,697
The escape plan.

612
00:18:25,047 --> 00:18:25,790
Angle three.

613
00:18:25,970 --> 00:18:26,655
The playbook.

614
00:18:26,835 --> 00:18:29,218
Ten steps to escape the broke loop,

615
00:18:29,218 --> 00:18:29,831
in order.

616
00:18:30,011 --> 00:18:31,306
You don't need to do them all

617
00:18:31,306 --> 00:18:31,752
this week.

618
00:18:31,932 --> 00:18:33,337
You need to do the next one.

619
00:18:33,687 --> 00:18:34,326
Step one.

620
00:18:34,506 --> 00:18:35,911
Find your real number.

621
00:18:36,091 --> 00:18:37,916
Look at the last three months of

622
00:18:37,916 --> 00:18:39,912
bank and card statements and add up

623
00:18:39,912 --> 00:18:41,338
everything that went out.

624
00:18:41,518 --> 00:18:42,854
Not what you think you spend.

625
00:18:43,034 --> 00:18:44,496
What you actually spent.

626
00:18:44,676 --> 00:18:45,988
Divide by three.

627
00:18:46,168 --> 00:18:48,641
That's your real monthly cost of living.

628
00:18:48,821 --> 00:18:50,482
Most people are surprised,

629
00:18:50,482 --> 00:18:52,269
and that surprise is useful.

630
00:18:52,619 --> 00:18:53,583
Step two.

631
00:18:53,763 --> 00:18:55,133
Build a starter cushion.

632
00:18:55,313 --> 00:18:56,819
Before anything fancy,

633
00:18:56,819 --> 00:18:58,530
get one thousand dollars,

634
00:18:58,530 --> 00:19:00,925
or one month of essential expenses,

635
00:19:00,925 --> 00:19:03,115
into a separate savings account.

636
00:19:03,295 --> 00:19:05,347
This is the wall between you and

637
00:19:05,347 --> 00:19:07,335
the next credit card emergency.

638
00:19:07,515 --> 00:19:09,072
It won't solve everything,

639
00:19:09,072 --> 00:19:11,167
but it breaks the cycle where every

640
00:19:11,167 --> 00:19:12,484
surprise becomes debt.

641
00:19:12,834 --> 00:19:14,367
How fast can you build it?

642
00:19:14,547 --> 00:19:16,078
At fifty dollars a week,

643
00:19:16,078 --> 00:19:18,565
you reach about one thousand dollars in

644
00:19:18,565 --> 00:19:19,330
five months.

645
00:19:19,510 --> 00:19:21,638
At one hundred dollars a week,

646
00:19:21,638 --> 00:19:22,773
about ten weeks.

647
00:19:22,953 --> 00:19:24,915
Pick a pace you can actually keep.

648
00:19:25,264 --> 00:19:26,158
Step three.

649
00:19:26,338 --> 00:19:27,650
Stop the bleeding.

650
00:19:27,830 --> 00:19:29,282
Cancel what you don't use.

651
00:19:29,462 --> 00:19:31,215
Most people have at least a few

652
00:19:31,215 --> 00:19:33,026
subscriptions they forgot about.

653
00:19:33,206 --> 00:19:35,956
Call your phone and internet providers and

654
00:19:35,956 --> 00:19:37,397
ask for a better rate.

655
00:19:37,577 --> 00:19:40,015
Shop your car insurance once a year.

656
00:19:40,195 --> 00:19:42,520
These are one time actions that save

657
00:19:42,520 --> 00:19:44,521
money every single month after,

658
00:19:44,521 --> 00:19:45,942
without any willpower.

659
00:19:46,292 --> 00:19:47,233
Step four.

660
00:19:47,413 --> 00:19:48,481
List every debt.

661
00:19:48,661 --> 00:19:49,999
Write down each balance,

662
00:19:49,999 --> 00:19:52,341
its interest rate and its minimum payment.

663
00:19:52,521 --> 00:19:53,647
Then choose a method.

664
00:19:53,827 --> 00:19:56,144
The avalanche method pays the highest rate

665
00:19:56,144 --> 00:19:58,019
first, which saves the most money.

666
00:19:58,199 --> 00:20:01,109
The snowball method pays the smallest balance

667
00:20:01,109 --> 00:20:03,307
first, which gives you quick wins.

668
00:20:03,487 --> 00:20:05,136
Both work if you stick with them.

669
00:20:05,316 --> 00:20:07,239
The best method is the one you'll

670
00:20:07,239 --> 00:20:08,172
actually follow.

671
00:20:08,521 --> 00:20:09,973
Here's the core difference.

672
00:20:10,153 --> 00:20:12,421
Avalanche: highest interest first,

673
00:20:12,421 --> 00:20:14,088
the least total interest.

674
00:20:14,268 --> 00:20:17,067
Snowball: smallest balance first,

675
00:20:17,067 --> 00:20:19,017
the fastest early wins.

676
00:20:19,197 --> 00:20:20,892
Pick one and keep going.

677
00:20:21,241 --> 00:20:22,368
Step five.

678
00:20:22,548 --> 00:20:23,848
Automate the good stuff.

679
00:20:24,028 --> 00:20:26,775
On payday, money should leave automatically for

680
00:20:26,775 --> 00:20:29,172
your cushion, your extra debt payment and

681
00:20:29,172 --> 00:20:30,808
eventually your investments.

682
00:20:30,988 --> 00:20:33,346
What's left is yours to spend without

683
00:20:33,346 --> 00:20:33,728
guilt.

684
00:20:33,908 --> 00:20:35,824
This flips the usual order.

685
00:20:36,004 --> 00:20:38,956
Instead of saving what's left after spending,

686
00:20:38,956 --> 00:20:41,251
you spend what's left after saving.

687
00:20:41,431 --> 00:20:43,313
That one switch is the heart of

688
00:20:43,313 --> 00:20:44,346
the whole escape.

689
00:20:44,695 --> 00:20:45,798
Step six.

690
00:20:45,978 --> 00:20:48,161
Add friction to impulse spending.

691
00:20:48,341 --> 00:20:49,827
Remove saved cards.

692
00:20:50,007 --> 00:20:52,143
Unsubscribe from store emails.

693
00:20:52,323 --> 00:20:54,278
Use a twenty four hour rule for

694
00:20:54,278 --> 00:20:55,981
anything that isn't a need.

695
00:20:56,161 --> 00:20:57,892
If you still want it tomorrow,

696
00:20:57,892 --> 00:20:58,181
fine.

697
00:20:58,361 --> 00:20:59,870
Very often, you won't.

698
00:21:00,050 --> 00:21:02,802
Small barriers beat big intentions.

699
00:21:03,151 --> 00:21:04,045
Step seven.

700
00:21:04,225 --> 00:21:05,398
Plan the irregular.

701
00:21:05,578 --> 00:21:07,378
Remember the optimism trap.

702
00:21:07,558 --> 00:21:09,026
Create a sinking fund,

703
00:21:09,026 --> 00:21:11,296
which is just a savings bucket for

704
00:21:11,296 --> 00:21:12,631
a known future cost.

705
00:21:12,811 --> 00:21:13,949
Car repairs.

706
00:21:14,129 --> 00:21:15,000
Holidays.

707
00:21:15,180 --> 00:21:16,132
Annual fees.

708
00:21:16,312 --> 00:21:16,985
Medical.

709
00:21:17,165 --> 00:21:18,760
Put a little in each month,

710
00:21:18,760 --> 00:21:20,119
so when the bill comes,

711
00:21:20,119 --> 00:21:21,182
it's already paid.

712
00:21:21,362 --> 00:21:23,698
This is how you stop getting surprised

713
00:21:23,698 --> 00:21:26,157
by things that weren't really surprises.

714
00:21:26,506 --> 00:21:27,273
Step eight.

715
00:21:27,453 --> 00:21:28,590
Grow your income.

716
00:21:28,770 --> 00:21:30,071
Cutting has a limit.

717
00:21:30,251 --> 00:21:31,110
Earning doesn't.

718
00:21:31,290 --> 00:21:33,017
Ask for a raise with evidence of

719
00:21:33,017 --> 00:21:34,204
what you've delivered.

720
00:21:34,384 --> 00:21:36,195
Learn a skill that pays more.

721
00:21:36,375 --> 00:21:38,047
Take on a side job for a

722
00:21:38,047 --> 00:21:40,276
set period with a specific goal,

723
00:21:40,276 --> 00:21:41,739
like clearing a card.

724
00:21:41,919 --> 00:21:43,222
And here's the key:

725
00:21:43,222 --> 00:21:45,759
send every extra dollar to your plan,

726
00:21:45,759 --> 00:21:47,062
not your lifestyle.

727
00:21:47,412 --> 00:21:48,272
Step nine.

728
00:21:48,452 --> 00:21:50,448
Build the full emergency fund.

729
00:21:50,628 --> 00:21:52,762
Once high interest debt is gone,

730
00:21:52,762 --> 00:21:54,895
grow the cushion to three to six

731
00:21:54,895 --> 00:21:56,828
months of essential expenses.

732
00:21:57,008 --> 00:21:59,110
More if your income is irregular.

733
00:21:59,290 --> 00:22:01,138
This is the moment the loop truly

734
00:22:01,138 --> 00:22:01,530
breaks.

735
00:22:01,710 --> 00:22:03,755
A job loss or a big repair

736
00:22:03,755 --> 00:22:05,170
becomes a problem,

737
00:22:05,170 --> 00:22:06,192
not a crisis.

738
00:22:06,541 --> 00:22:07,470
Step ten.

739
00:22:07,650 --> 00:22:09,891
Start investing for the long term.

740
00:22:10,071 --> 00:22:12,774
Take the same automatic payments that used

741
00:22:12,774 --> 00:22:13,675
to go to debt,

742
00:22:13,675 --> 00:22:16,121
and point them at a retirement account

743
00:22:16,121 --> 00:22:18,117
and low cost diversified funds.

744
00:22:18,297 --> 00:22:19,903
Get any employer match first,

745
00:22:19,903 --> 00:22:21,675
because that's part of your pay.

746
00:22:21,855 --> 00:22:23,829
Then let time do the heavy lifting.

747
00:22:24,178 --> 00:22:27,058
Here's why step ten matters so much.

748
00:22:27,238 --> 00:22:29,440
Say you were paying four hundred dollars

749
00:22:29,440 --> 00:22:30,651
a month toward a card.

750
00:22:30,831 --> 00:22:31,794
Once it's gone,

751
00:22:31,794 --> 00:22:34,426
you invest that same four hundred dollars

752
00:22:34,426 --> 00:22:36,608
a month at a seven percent average

753
00:22:36,608 --> 00:22:37,507
yearly return.

754
00:22:37,687 --> 00:22:38,990
Over twenty five years,

755
00:22:38,990 --> 00:22:41,425
here's roughly where that habit could lead.

756
00:22:41,775 --> 00:22:43,313
That's an illustration,

757
00:22:43,313 --> 00:22:44,248
not a promise.

758
00:22:44,428 --> 00:22:45,589
But look at the shape of it.

759
00:22:45,769 --> 00:22:47,571
The payment you were already used to

760
00:22:47,571 --> 00:22:49,624
making becomes the engine of your future.

761
00:22:49,804 --> 00:22:51,456
You don't need to feel richer to

762
00:22:51,456 --> 00:22:51,766
start.

763
00:22:51,946 --> 00:22:53,609
You just need to keep making the

764
00:22:53,609 --> 00:22:55,220
same payment to a better place.

765
00:22:55,569 --> 00:22:56,487
Ten steps.

766
00:22:56,667 --> 00:22:57,928
Find your number,

767
00:22:57,928 --> 00:22:59,115
build a cushion,

768
00:22:59,115 --> 00:23:00,227
stop the leaks,

769
00:23:00,227 --> 00:23:01,414
list your debts,

770
00:23:01,414 --> 00:23:03,121
automate, add friction,

771
00:23:03,121 --> 00:23:04,530
plan the irregular,

772
00:23:04,530 --> 00:23:07,275
earn more, finish the emergency fund,

773
00:23:07,275 --> 00:23:08,091
and invest.

774
00:23:08,271 --> 00:23:09,362
That's the whole escape.

775
00:23:09,712 --> 00:23:11,761
Let me make this real with three

776
00:23:11,761 --> 00:23:12,209
people.

777
00:23:12,389 --> 00:23:15,514
They're examples, but you'll probably recognize at

778
00:23:15,514 --> 00:23:16,139
least one.

779
00:23:16,488 --> 00:23:18,073
Jasmine, twenty nine,

780
00:23:18,073 --> 00:23:20,337
earns a decent salary in a big

781
00:23:20,337 --> 00:23:20,714
city.

782
00:23:20,894 --> 00:23:22,272
She's never missed rent,

783
00:23:22,272 --> 00:23:24,051
but she's never had more than a

784
00:23:24,051 --> 00:23:25,945
few hundred dollars saved either.

785
00:23:26,125 --> 00:23:29,283
Her problem is lifestyle creep and comparison.

786
00:23:29,463 --> 00:23:32,063
When she tracked three months of spending,

787
00:23:32,063 --> 00:23:34,354
she found about six hundred dollars a

788
00:23:34,354 --> 00:23:35,407
month in takeout,

789
00:23:35,407 --> 00:23:38,379
rideshares and subscriptions she barely noticed.

790
00:23:38,729 --> 00:23:40,366
She didn't cut everything.

791
00:23:40,546 --> 00:23:41,696
She cut half.

792
00:23:41,876 --> 00:23:44,562
Three hundred dollars a month moved automatically

793
00:23:44,562 --> 00:23:45,823
into savings on payday.

794
00:23:46,003 --> 00:23:47,677
In a little over three months she

795
00:23:47,677 --> 00:23:48,894
had her starter cushion.

796
00:23:49,074 --> 00:23:50,050
A year later,

797
00:23:50,050 --> 00:23:52,302
she had a real emergency fund.

798
00:23:52,482 --> 00:23:54,630
Same job, same city.

799
00:23:54,810 --> 00:23:56,296
The difference was the order.

800
00:23:56,476 --> 00:23:58,345
Save first, then spend.

801
00:23:58,694 --> 00:24:00,165
Marcus, thirty eight,

802
00:24:00,165 --> 00:24:02,966
has two kids and about fourteen thousand

803
00:24:02,966 --> 00:24:05,347
dollars across three credit cards.

804
00:24:05,527 --> 00:24:07,501
Every month he pays the minimums and

805
00:24:07,501 --> 00:24:09,091
wonders where the money goes.

806
00:24:09,271 --> 00:24:10,931
His problem is the debt loop.

807
00:24:11,111 --> 00:24:13,562
Every surprise expense lands on a card,

808
00:24:13,562 --> 00:24:16,139
and the interest keeps the balance stuck.

809
00:24:16,488 --> 00:24:19,259
Marcus built a one thousand dollar cushion

810
00:24:19,259 --> 00:24:22,029
first, so new emergencies stopped going on

811
00:24:22,029 --> 00:24:22,688
the cards.

812
00:24:22,868 --> 00:24:24,772
Then he listed his debts and used

813
00:24:24,772 --> 00:24:26,734
the avalanche, highest rate first.

814
00:24:26,914 --> 00:24:28,819
He picked up weekend shifts for a

815
00:24:28,819 --> 00:24:30,319
year and sent all of it to

816
00:24:30,319 --> 00:24:30,838
the debt.

817
00:24:31,018 --> 00:24:32,950
It took about two and a half

818
00:24:32,950 --> 00:24:33,364
years.

819
00:24:33,544 --> 00:24:35,778
Then he pointed the same monthly payment

820
00:24:35,778 --> 00:24:36,504
at investing.

821
00:24:36,854 --> 00:24:38,309
And Linda, fifty two,

822
00:24:38,309 --> 00:24:40,180
earns well and has no debt,

823
00:24:40,180 --> 00:24:43,228
but has almost nothing saved for retirement.

824
00:24:43,408 --> 00:24:45,161
Her problem is the waiting trap.

825
00:24:45,341 --> 00:24:47,141
She always meant to start.

826
00:24:47,321 --> 00:24:48,784
Now she's catching up.

827
00:24:48,964 --> 00:24:51,221
People fifty and older can make extra

828
00:24:51,221 --> 00:24:54,026
catch up contributions to retirement accounts,

829
00:24:54,026 --> 00:24:55,856
and her income is at its peak,

830
00:24:55,856 --> 00:24:57,625
so she can save a high share.

831
00:24:57,805 --> 00:24:59,512
She can't change the past.

832
00:24:59,692 --> 00:25:02,164
She can change the next fifteen years.

833
00:25:02,514 --> 00:25:03,815
Three different people.

834
00:25:03,995 --> 00:25:05,376
Three different traps.

835
00:25:05,556 --> 00:25:06,915
One shared solution.

836
00:25:07,095 --> 00:25:09,725
They stopped relying on willpower and built

837
00:25:09,725 --> 00:25:10,276
a system.

838
00:25:10,456 --> 00:25:13,012
A cushion, an automatic transfer,

839
00:25:13,012 --> 00:25:15,181
a clear order of priorities.

840
00:25:15,361 --> 00:25:16,917
The system did the work on the

841
00:25:16,917 --> 00:25:18,473
days they didn't feel like it.

842
00:25:18,822 --> 00:25:21,212
Let me tell you what escaping actually

843
00:25:21,212 --> 00:25:22,909
feels like, stage by stage.

844
00:25:23,089 --> 00:25:24,656
Because it's not one moment.

845
00:25:24,836 --> 00:25:26,799
It's a series of small ones.

846
00:25:27,148 --> 00:25:28,112
Stage one.

847
00:25:28,292 --> 00:25:29,151
The first month.

848
00:25:29,331 --> 00:25:31,096
It feels uncomfortable.

849
00:25:31,276 --> 00:25:33,180
You're looking at numbers you avoided.

850
00:25:33,360 --> 00:25:35,093
You're saying no to things you used

851
00:25:35,093 --> 00:25:35,786
to say yes to.

852
00:25:35,966 --> 00:25:37,743
Nothing in your life looks different yet.

853
00:25:37,923 --> 00:25:39,937
This is the stage where most people

854
00:25:39,937 --> 00:25:42,010
quit, because the effort is real and

855
00:25:42,010 --> 00:25:43,391
the reward is invisible.

856
00:25:43,741 --> 00:25:45,053
Stage two.

857
00:25:45,233 --> 00:25:47,346
Around month three to six.

858
00:25:47,526 --> 00:25:49,117
The starter cushion is there.

859
00:25:49,297 --> 00:25:51,316
A bill arrives that would have gone

860
00:25:51,316 --> 00:25:51,893
on a card,

861
00:25:51,893 --> 00:25:53,047
and you just pay it.

862
00:25:53,227 --> 00:25:54,771
That's the first real win.

863
00:25:54,951 --> 00:25:56,658
Your stress drops a little.

864
00:25:56,838 --> 00:25:59,049
You start checking your accounts because you

865
00:25:59,049 --> 00:26:00,959
want to, not because you're scared to.

866
00:26:01,309 --> 00:26:02,459
Stage three.

867
00:26:02,639 --> 00:26:04,427
Somewhere in the first two years.

868
00:26:04,607 --> 00:26:06,801
The last high interest debt is gone.

869
00:26:06,981 --> 00:26:08,600
The money that used to go to

870
00:26:08,600 --> 00:26:10,162
interest is suddenly yours.

871
00:26:10,342 --> 00:26:11,798
It feels like a raise,

872
00:26:11,798 --> 00:26:12,989
because it is one.

873
00:26:13,169 --> 00:26:15,279
This is often when people realize the

874
00:26:15,279 --> 00:26:17,275
system works, and they start to get

875
00:26:17,275 --> 00:26:18,359
a little ambitious.

876
00:26:18,708 --> 00:26:19,707
Stage four.

877
00:26:19,887 --> 00:26:22,210
The full emergency fund and the first

878
00:26:22,210 --> 00:26:22,963
investments.

879
00:26:23,143 --> 00:26:25,474
You stop thinking of yourself as someone

880
00:26:25,474 --> 00:26:26,174
who's broke.

881
00:26:26,354 --> 00:26:27,357
A job change,

882
00:26:27,357 --> 00:26:29,673
a car repair or a medical bill

883
00:26:29,673 --> 00:26:31,140
becomes a decision,

884
00:26:31,140 --> 00:26:32,298
not a disaster.

885
00:26:32,478 --> 00:26:34,969
And quietly, the investments start to grow

886
00:26:34,969 --> 00:26:35,740
on their own.

887
00:26:35,920 --> 00:26:38,084
That's the moment the loop is officially

888
00:26:38,084 --> 00:26:38,463
broken.

889
00:26:38,643 --> 00:26:40,106
You're no longer escaping.

890
00:26:40,286 --> 00:26:41,168
You're building.

891
00:26:41,518 --> 00:26:42,844
Before the questions,

892
00:26:42,844 --> 00:26:44,990
five myths that keep people stuck.

893
00:26:45,170 --> 00:26:45,855
Myth one.

894
00:26:46,035 --> 00:26:47,616
You need a high income to build

895
00:26:47,616 --> 00:26:47,974
wealth.

896
00:26:48,154 --> 00:26:50,393
Income helps, but the gap between what

897
00:26:50,393 --> 00:26:52,456
you earn and what you spend matters

898
00:26:52,456 --> 00:26:52,751
more.

899
00:26:52,931 --> 00:26:53,825
Myth two.

900
00:26:54,005 --> 00:26:55,712
Budgets are about restriction.

901
00:26:55,892 --> 00:26:57,624
A good budget is just a plan

902
00:26:57,624 --> 00:26:59,666
that tells your money where to go

903
00:26:59,666 --> 00:27:00,965
before it disappears.

904
00:27:01,315 --> 00:27:02,639
Myth three.

905
00:27:02,819 --> 00:27:04,560
Small amounts don't matter.

906
00:27:04,740 --> 00:27:07,797
Small amounts, repeated for years and compounded,

907
00:27:07,797 --> 00:27:10,418
are how most ordinary people build wealth.

908
00:27:10,598 --> 00:27:11,445
Myth four.

909
00:27:11,625 --> 00:27:13,239
Investing is gambling.

910
00:27:13,419 --> 00:27:15,675
Picking hot stocks on a hunch can

911
00:27:15,675 --> 00:27:15,880
be.

912
00:27:16,060 --> 00:27:18,965
Owning broad, diversified funds for decades is

913
00:27:18,965 --> 00:27:20,417
a very different thing,

914
00:27:20,417 --> 00:27:22,248
though it still carries risk.

915
00:27:22,428 --> 00:27:23,543
And myth five.

916
00:27:23,723 --> 00:27:24,663
It's too late.

917
00:27:24,843 --> 00:27:26,167
It's later than it could have been.

918
00:27:26,347 --> 00:27:28,049
It's never too late to make the

919
00:27:28,049 --> 00:27:29,970
next ten years better than the last

920
00:27:29,970 --> 00:27:30,190
ten.

921
00:27:30,539 --> 00:27:32,547
Now the questions people ask me most

922
00:27:32,547 --> 00:27:34,220
about escaping the broke loop.

923
00:27:34,569 --> 00:27:35,394
Question one.

924
00:27:35,574 --> 00:27:37,642
Should I save or pay off debt

925
00:27:37,642 --> 00:27:38,070
first?

926
00:27:38,250 --> 00:27:39,291
For most people,

927
00:27:39,291 --> 00:27:41,632
a small starter cushion comes first,

928
00:27:41,632 --> 00:27:44,299
so new emergencies don't create new debt.

929
00:27:44,479 --> 00:27:47,178
Then attack high interest debt aggressively,

930
00:27:47,178 --> 00:27:49,326
because paying off a card at twenty

931
00:27:49,326 --> 00:27:51,964
percent is like earning a guaranteed twenty

932
00:27:51,964 --> 00:27:52,884
percent return.

933
00:27:53,064 --> 00:27:55,857
Then build the full emergency fund,

934
00:27:55,857 --> 00:27:56,814
then invest.

935
00:27:56,994 --> 00:28:00,159
One exception: if your employer matches retirement

936
00:28:00,159 --> 00:28:02,943
contributions, many people grab at least the

937
00:28:02,943 --> 00:28:05,412
match early, because that's free money.

938
00:28:05,761 --> 00:28:06,702
Question two.

939
00:28:06,882 --> 00:28:09,018
What if I truly can't cut anything?

940
00:28:09,198 --> 00:28:11,702
Sometimes the budget really is tight,

941
00:28:11,702 --> 00:28:13,462
and the problem is income,

942
00:28:13,462 --> 00:28:14,341
not spending.

943
00:28:14,521 --> 00:28:15,396
If that's you,

944
00:28:15,396 --> 00:28:16,646
focus on step eight.

945
00:28:16,826 --> 00:28:17,960
Ask for a raise,

946
00:28:17,960 --> 00:28:19,944
look at better paying roles,

947
00:28:19,944 --> 00:28:20,936
learn a skill,

948
00:28:20,936 --> 00:28:23,416
or add a temporary side income with

949
00:28:23,416 --> 00:28:24,338
a clear goal.

950
00:28:24,518 --> 00:28:27,392
Also check whether you qualify for programs

951
00:28:27,392 --> 00:28:29,196
that lower essential costs.

952
00:28:29,376 --> 00:28:30,537
There's no shame in it.

953
00:28:30,887 --> 00:28:32,014
Question three.

954
00:28:32,194 --> 00:28:34,571
Is a debt consolidation loan a good

955
00:28:34,571 --> 00:28:34,910
idea?

956
00:28:35,090 --> 00:28:35,706
It can be,

957
00:28:35,706 --> 00:28:37,676
if it gives you a lower interest

958
00:28:37,676 --> 00:28:39,769
rate and you stop using the cards.

959
00:28:39,949 --> 00:28:42,743
The danger is that people consolidate,

960
00:28:42,743 --> 00:28:45,317
feel relief, and then run the cards

961
00:28:45,317 --> 00:28:45,905
back up.

962
00:28:46,085 --> 00:28:47,774
Now they have the loan and the

963
00:28:47,774 --> 00:28:48,337
card debt.

964
00:28:48,517 --> 00:28:49,669
If you consolidate,

965
00:28:49,669 --> 00:28:50,942
close the loop first:

966
00:28:50,942 --> 00:28:53,245
build the cushion and remove the cards

967
00:28:53,245 --> 00:28:55,123
from your wallet and your apps.

968
00:28:55,473 --> 00:28:56,518
Question four.

969
00:28:56,698 --> 00:28:58,358
How long does it take to stop

970
00:28:58,358 --> 00:28:59,160
feeling broke?

971
00:28:59,340 --> 00:29:01,903
Most people feel a real difference within

972
00:29:01,903 --> 00:29:03,903
three to six months of starting,

973
00:29:03,903 --> 00:29:06,279
usually when the starter cushion is in

974
00:29:06,279 --> 00:29:06,654
place.

975
00:29:06,834 --> 00:29:09,103
The first time a surprise bill arrives

976
00:29:09,103 --> 00:29:11,073
and you just pay it from savings,

977
00:29:11,073 --> 00:29:11,969
without a card,

978
00:29:11,969 --> 00:29:13,103
is a turning point.

979
00:29:13,283 --> 00:29:15,418
Many people say that feeling is worth

980
00:29:15,418 --> 00:29:16,975
more than the money itself.

981
00:29:17,325 --> 00:29:18,301
Question five.

982
00:29:18,481 --> 00:29:19,595
What about my partner?

983
00:29:19,775 --> 00:29:21,556
Money is one of the most common

984
00:29:21,556 --> 00:29:23,107
things couples argue about.

985
00:29:23,287 --> 00:29:25,598
The escape works best as a team.

986
00:29:25,778 --> 00:29:27,222
Sit down on a calm day,

987
00:29:27,222 --> 00:29:28,728
share your real numbers,

988
00:29:28,728 --> 00:29:30,863
and agree on the next single step.

989
00:29:31,043 --> 00:29:32,204
Not the whole plan.

990
00:29:32,384 --> 00:29:33,289
One step.

991
00:29:33,469 --> 00:29:35,664
Then celebrate it together when it's done.

992
00:29:36,013 --> 00:29:37,093
Question six.

993
00:29:37,273 --> 00:29:38,875
Should I use buy now pay later?

994
00:29:39,055 --> 00:29:41,566
It's convenient, and some plans charge no

995
00:29:41,566 --> 00:29:43,281
interest if you pay on time.

996
00:29:43,461 --> 00:29:45,808
But it makes spending painless again,

997
00:29:45,808 --> 00:29:47,901
and several plans at once can add

998
00:29:47,901 --> 00:29:49,359
up quickly and quietly.

999
00:29:49,539 --> 00:29:51,755
If you're trying to escape the broke

1000
00:29:51,755 --> 00:29:53,170
loop, a simple rule is:

1001
00:29:53,170 --> 00:29:54,955
if you can't afford it today,

1002
00:29:54,955 --> 00:29:56,494
don't split it into four.

1003
00:29:56,843 --> 00:29:57,714
Question eight.

1004
00:29:57,894 --> 00:29:59,800
Does my credit score really matter if

1005
00:29:59,800 --> 00:30:00,727
I'm not borrowing?

1006
00:30:00,907 --> 00:30:02,695
Yes, more than people think.

1007
00:30:02,875 --> 00:30:05,140
Landlords, insurers in many states,

1008
00:30:05,140 --> 00:30:07,404
and even some employers may look at

1009
00:30:07,404 --> 00:30:08,634
credit information.

1010
00:30:08,814 --> 00:30:11,663
A better score can mean lower deposits,

1011
00:30:11,663 --> 00:30:13,783
lower rates and more options.

1012
00:30:13,963 --> 00:30:15,444
The basics are simple:

1013
00:30:15,444 --> 00:30:16,992
pay every bill on time,

1014
00:30:16,992 --> 00:30:19,752
keep card balances low compared with your

1015
00:30:19,752 --> 00:30:22,512
limits, and check your credit reports for

1016
00:30:22,512 --> 00:30:22,984
errors.

1017
00:30:23,164 --> 00:30:25,196
You can get your reports for free

1018
00:30:25,196 --> 00:30:26,613
from the official site,

1019
00:30:26,613 --> 00:30:28,400
annual credit report dot com.

1020
00:30:28,749 --> 00:30:30,003
And question seven.

1021
00:30:30,183 --> 00:30:31,948
What's the very first thing I should

1022
00:30:31,948 --> 00:30:32,389
do today?

1023
00:30:32,569 --> 00:30:34,322
Open your banking app and look.

1024
00:30:34,502 --> 00:30:35,013
That's it.

1025
00:30:35,193 --> 00:30:36,526
Look at your balance,

1026
00:30:36,526 --> 00:30:37,922
your last thirty days,

1027
00:30:37,922 --> 00:30:38,874
and your debts.

1028
00:30:39,054 --> 00:30:41,263
The ostrich effect is the first trap

1029
00:30:41,263 --> 00:30:43,840
to break, because every other step depends

1030
00:30:43,840 --> 00:30:45,497
on knowing where you stand.

1031
00:30:45,847 --> 00:30:47,287
Here's a simple first week.

1032
00:30:47,467 --> 00:30:49,476
Day one, look at your accounts.

1033
00:30:49,656 --> 00:30:52,326
Day two, add up last month's spending.

1034
00:30:52,506 --> 00:30:54,901
Day three, cancel two things you don't

1035
00:30:54,901 --> 00:30:55,153
use.

1036
00:30:55,333 --> 00:30:57,819
Day four, open a separate savings account

1037
00:30:57,819 --> 00:30:59,153
if you don't have one.

1038
00:30:59,333 --> 00:31:02,187
Day five, set up an automatic transfer,

1039
00:31:02,187 --> 00:31:03,431
even ten dollars.

1040
00:31:03,611 --> 00:31:05,915
Day six, list your debts by interest

1041
00:31:05,915 --> 00:31:06,235
rate.

1042
00:31:06,415 --> 00:31:08,005
Day seven, rest.

1043
00:31:08,185 --> 00:31:10,354
You've already done more than most people

1044
00:31:10,354 --> 00:31:11,041
do in a year.

1045
00:31:11,391 --> 00:31:13,349
None of these steps need a big

1046
00:31:13,349 --> 00:31:13,806
income.

1047
00:31:13,986 --> 00:31:15,569
None of them take more than an

1048
00:31:15,569 --> 00:31:15,832
hour.

1049
00:31:16,012 --> 00:31:17,731
But together they flip the system.

1050
00:31:17,911 --> 00:31:20,299
You stop being the person things happen

1051
00:31:20,299 --> 00:31:22,381
to, and start being the person who

1052
00:31:22,381 --> 00:31:24,157
decides where the money goes.

1053
00:31:24,506 --> 00:31:25,354
Quick recap.

1054
00:31:25,534 --> 00:31:28,005
The numbers: about four in ten adults

1055
00:31:28,005 --> 00:31:31,010
couldn't cover a four hundred dollar surprise

1056
00:31:31,010 --> 00:31:33,682
with cash, Americans owe over a trillion

1057
00:31:33,682 --> 00:31:35,285
dollars on credit cards,

1058
00:31:35,285 --> 00:31:37,822
and the average household saves only a

1059
00:31:37,822 --> 00:31:39,559
small slice of its income.

1060
00:31:39,739 --> 00:31:40,687
Broke is normal,

1061
00:31:40,687 --> 00:31:42,049
but it's not permanent.

1062
00:31:42,399 --> 00:31:45,778
The psychology: scarcity tunnels your vision,

1063
00:31:45,778 --> 00:31:47,355
today beats tomorrow,

1064
00:31:47,355 --> 00:31:50,132
comparison spending, lifestyle creep,

1065
00:31:50,132 --> 00:31:52,985
mental accounting, the ostrich effect,

1066
00:31:52,985 --> 00:31:55,313
painless credit, optimism bias,

1067
00:31:55,313 --> 00:31:58,166
identity, and waiting for more income.

1068
00:31:58,346 --> 00:32:00,311
Ten traps, all human,

1069
00:32:00,311 --> 00:32:01,434
all fixable.

1070
00:32:01,784 --> 00:32:03,151
And the playbook:

1071
00:32:03,151 --> 00:32:04,518
find your number,

1072
00:32:04,518 --> 00:32:05,804
build a cushion,

1073
00:32:05,804 --> 00:32:07,010
stop the leaks,

1074
00:32:07,010 --> 00:32:08,296
list your debts,

1075
00:32:08,296 --> 00:32:10,145
automate, add friction,

1076
00:32:10,145 --> 00:32:11,672
plan the irregular,

1077
00:32:11,672 --> 00:32:14,646
earn more, finish the emergency fund,

1078
00:32:14,646 --> 00:32:15,531
and invest.

1079
00:32:15,711 --> 00:32:16,961
One step at a time,

1080
00:32:16,961 --> 00:32:17,882
in that order.

1081
00:32:18,231 --> 00:32:19,911
If you want the whole plan in

1082
00:32:19,911 --> 00:32:21,939
one place, from broke to your first

1083
00:32:21,939 --> 00:32:23,618
million, it's in my playbook.

1084
00:32:23,798 --> 00:32:25,401
The link is in the description.

1085
00:32:25,581 --> 00:32:27,595
And if you're building your cushion right

1086
00:32:27,595 --> 00:32:29,315
now, watch my video on what changes

1087
00:32:29,315 --> 00:32:31,280
when you have twenty thousand dollars in

1088
00:32:31,280 --> 00:32:31,722
the bank.

1089
00:32:31,902 --> 00:32:33,458
That's where this road leads.

1090
00:32:33,638 --> 00:32:34,938
I'm Ryan Rover.

1091
00:32:35,118 --> 00:32:36,291
See you in the next one.
